(p-578) ﴿And they give food, in spite of their love for it,﴾ that is, in spite of their love for food, along with their desire for it and their need of it; or, out of love for God;

﴿to the needy,﴾ the poor one unable to earn a living;

﴿and the orphan,﴾ the young one who has no father;

﴿and the captive,﴾ the one taken captive, the slave, or others. Then they gave the reason for feeding them, saying:

(p-583) ﴿And you do not will﴾ — taking the way to Allah — and with the ya' (yaʼ, i.e., reading تَشاءُونَ as يَشاؤُونَ with the initial ya') it is read by Makki, Shami and Abu 'Amr. The position (maḥall) of ﴿unless Allah wills﴾ is the accusative (naṣb) as an adverbial of time (ẓarf), meaning: except at the time of Allah's willing. Allah only wills that for those whom He knows to choose it. It has also been said that it refers to the generality of His will regarding obedience and disobedience, disbelief and belief, and so it is a proof for us against the Mu'tazilah.

﴿Indeed, Allah is ever Knowing﴾ — of what will come from them of states;

﴿Wise﴾ — correct in sayings and deeds.
